{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>From my father's house and from my native land.</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 24:7): \"From my father's house\" — from Haran; \"and from my native land\" — from Ur of the Chaldeans. If so, \"you shall go to my land and to my native place\" would mean Ur of the Chaldeans — far be it, far be it, that the holy seed should be mingled with the children of Ham the wicked. Perhaps one might say that he had family there from the seed of Shem, but the servant went to Haran, as his master had spoken. Or perhaps the Rav considers that his native land is Ur of the Chaldeans, but \"my native place\" (מולדתי) means my family, and \"my land\" means the land in which he sojourned. But all these explanations are void, for here he says, \"and you shall take a wife for my son from there,\" and furthermore, because he dwelt in the land of Canaan for many days, longer than in Haran — why should Haran be called his land merely because he sojourned there for days? Rather, \"to my land and to my native place\" — my land, where my birthplace is, for there he was established, and from there were his fathers from of old, and this has already been explained (above, Genesis 11:28; 12:1). And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 59:10): \"From my father's house\" — this is his father's household; \"and from my native land\" — this is my dwelling-place. And it is possible that the sense of \"to my land and to my native place\" is: to my land and to my family, for even among the people of his land he desires only his family. And so, \"and you shall take a wife for my son from there\" is an allusion to \"from my father's house.\" And likewise the servant says (Genesis 24:40), \"and you shall take a wife for my son from my family and from my father's house,\" and he says (ibid. 41), \"when you come to my family\" — or perhaps the servant said this to honor them, so that they would listen to him." "<b>Who spoke to me.</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 24:7): \"for my sake,\" like \"concerning me,\" and likewise all instances of לי and לו and להם attached to an expression of דיבור (speaking) are interpreted with the sense of על (concerning), for the expression of דיבור (speech) only fittingly takes אלי, אליו, אליהם (to me, to him, to them), and their Targum is עמי, עמיה, עמהון (with me, with him, with them). But with the expression of אמירה (saying), לי and לו and להם are appropriate. And in the section of Jacob's departure, he brought proof from what is written there (Genesis 28:15), \"which I spoke to you (לך),\" for behold, with Jacob He had not spoken previously. But this distinction is not correct, for behold, we find (Exodus 32:34), \"And now go, lead the people to the place of which I spoke to you (לך)\"; \"And the Chaldeans spoke to the king in Aramaic\" (Daniel 2:4); \"And you shall dispossess them and destroy them quickly, as YHWH your Elohim spoke to you (לך)\" (Deuteronomy 9:3). And likewise the expression of אמירה (saying) is found with both: (Exodus 3:13) \"And they will say to me (לי), 'What is His name?' What shall I say to them (אליהם)?\" And \"which I spoke to you (לך)\" means \"which I have now spoken to you, that I will give you the land and to your seed, and I will bless you.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "In the phrase from Rashi, לצרכי means 'for my sake'; the following עלי means 'concerning me.' Rashi's grammatical claim is that לי after דיבור means 'concerning me,' not 'to me.' Ramban rejects this distinction."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:02.070001564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}